WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
Shane Stanley, Filmmaker/Author/Instructor:&nbsp;
I’d rather have four or five films under my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:05.540
belt in five years than talking about&nbsp;
making one that's just me personally. &nbsp;

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.720
Film Courage: When you were making&nbsp;
movies with Charlie Sheen, at that time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.920
how much did it cost to make them and how much&nbsp;
were the distributors paying you for the movies?

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:22.400
Shane: Back when we were doing those we were an&nbsp;
independent company that decided to do its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.120
content. It's the early working with some&nbsp;
of the majors but getting a real taste of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.880
independent filmmaking. We were really in&nbsp;
a fortunate situation because Charlie was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.960
movie star. He had just come off things like&nbsp;
Terminal Velocity and Shadow Conspiracy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:44.080
The Arrival and his incredible catalog&nbsp;
of work. We had the Avi Lerners&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.520
coming to us saying we'll give you up&nbsp;
to 10-to-12 million to go make a movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.760
They weren't distributors that were buying&nbsp;
them. They were people like Avi that obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:02.720
(smart businessman) he would cast it with real&nbsp;
players and sell it at Cannes [Film Festival] or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.760
AFM [American Film Market] before we even shot&nbsp;
a foot of film. I remember we went to Cannes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:12.320
for the 50th anniversary and they put&nbsp;
us up at the Hôtel du Cap and we had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.480
press conference and it was mock artwork of&nbsp;
the film with Charlie Sheen, Mark Dacascos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:24.480
Joe Lando, Martin Sheen and they pre-sold&nbsp;
that thing globally I'm sure for a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.360
than they gave us to make it. The film did&nbsp;
very well. Miramax Dimension actually ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:36.000
up picking it up and they made some good&nbsp;
TV deals on it. We made the film for 12,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.040
we actually made it for nine because we shot it in&nbsp;
Arizona. At the time they had a 30 percent rebate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:47.040
so we got I think 3 million back in actual cash&nbsp;
rebate (wasn't tax incentives, it was cash rebate)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.040
and that's how movies used to be made especially&nbsp;
in such a huge home video market. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:59.280
the height of Blockbuster and Hollywood&nbsp;
video and all those great brick and mortar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.880
Ma and Pa stores were just they they were printing&nbsp;
money because of the home video market and DVDs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.640
coming out blu-ray hadn't hit yet so they were&nbsp;
giving us a lot of money. We were making some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.840
these indie films for eight to 12 million. Those&nbsp;
were good times, those were really good times.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:16.880
Film Courage: How much easier was&nbsp;
