you from getting the movie made so for me I don't&nbsp;
think about what a budget has to be I think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.760
I always put my investor first I always think&nbsp;
what's going to put he or she at the lowest risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.360
how do we execute this to the best of our ability&nbsp;
so we can out kick our punt coverage we can now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.600
punch our weight class puts put it all on the&nbsp;
screen and wow people for a fraction of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:21.120
people think we actually made it for that's just&nbsp;
my mentality doesn't mean it's right for everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:27.280
so when I look at a project when an investor&nbsp;
calls me and says we're ready to go or I call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:31.520
them and say I'm ready to go what's your comfort&nbsp;
level for the next season and they tell me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:40.320
I say okay this is how much we have what can we do&nbsp;
with this I go into it bass backwards and I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.320
I watch filmmakers time and time again not&nbsp;
get movies made because they've set some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.160
some budget in their mind and unless&nbsp;
you're being given the money by a studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:56.480
or a network who is really governing that&nbsp;
that's what the budget has to be my father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.680
tried for four years to get desperate passage&nbsp;
made he had a million dollar budget back in 1980&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:08.640
well it started in 82. so a million dollars&nbsp;
was a lot of money back then finally in 1985-86&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.520
he got 25 grand to go make the movie that was&nbsp;
cutting really short to the chase on this story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:20.720
and my my mother my father and I with a couple&nbsp;
of tight filmmaker friends we we huddled together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.200
and figured out how we were going to do it and&nbsp;
I don't think the film would have been at all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.520
different if we had had the million versus the&nbsp;
25. I don't think a frame of the film would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:35.680
been any different and the success that that film&nbsp;
had it taught me how to make movies for nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:43.200
and what it launched ultimately gridiron gang&nbsp;
and a lot of seven other movies of the week that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:49.200
collectively did very well that all came from a 25&nbsp;
000 let's do this from a million dollar budget so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:55.520
for me I always tell a filmmaker what do you&nbsp;
have access to what are your means if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.240
going to write the check yourself what can you&nbsp;
